Subject: [PATCH] extensionManagement: dedupe identical builtins across
 simultaneous remote+web management servers

Upstream project: microsoft/vscode
Target file:      src/vs/workbench/services/extensionManagement/common/extensionManagementService.ts
Target symbol:    ExtensionManagementService.getInstalled()
Confirmed present at: microsoft/vscode @ main (fetched 2026-08-27) --
  i.e. NOT fixed by any release up to and including current upstream
  HEAD, so no version upgrade resolves this (see README.md Part 3/4).
Also confirmed present, byte-for-byte identical logic, in the pinned
  codercom/code-server:4.133.0 image's bundled VS Code 1.133.0
  (commit d2f7a122522456b351e9b3ddd39e4f3fb9fd5318).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
ROOT CAUSE (see README.md for the full trace)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

`ExtensionManagementService.getInstalled()` queries every configured
management server (`localExtensionManagementServer`,
`remoteExtensionManagementServer`, `webExtensionManagementServer`) in
parallel and concatenates their results verbatim:

    async getInstalled(type?, profileLocation?, productVersion?) {
        const result: ILocalExtension[] = [];
        await Promise.all(this.servers.map(async server => {
            const installed = await server.extensionManagementService
                .getInstalled(type, profileLocation, productVersion);
            if (server === this.getWorkspaceExtensionsServer()) {
                const workspaceExtensions =
                    await this.getInstalledWorkspaceExtensions(true);
                installed.push(...workspaceExtensions);
            }
            result.push(...installed);
        }));
        return result;
    }

Every downstream consumer of this flattened list (notably
`ExtensionsManager` in
`extensionManagement/browser/extensionEnablementService.ts`, and
`AbstractExtensionService`'s `_handleDeltaExtensions`/`deltaExtensions`
pipeline) intentionally treats entries as distinct per
`(identifier, managementServer)` pair -- correct in general, since a
user's local, remote, and web servers can each genuinely hold
different, independently-installed extensions.

That assumption breaks for a *builtin* extension shipped with a
`browser` entry point in its manifest (e.g.
`vscode.typescript-language-features`) when a single deployment
configures BOTH `remoteExtensionManagementServer` AND
`webExtensionManagementServer` at once -- exactly code-server's own
model, since it always runs a real remote extension host *and* always
serves VS Code Web's static web-extension assets from the same origin.
Both servers correctly report the SAME physical builtin as "installed"
under their own scope, `getInstalled()` faithfully returns both as
separate `ILocalExtension` entries with identical identifier/version/
uuid but different `extensionLocation`/`targetPlatform`, and nothing
downstream ever collapses them back into one.

The collision is latent until something re-evaluates the full
extension list and re-emits both entries together in a single
`deltaExtensions` `toAdd` array -- which
`updateExtensionsEnablementsWhenWorkspaceTrustChanges()` does the first
time workspace trust transitions to `true` (any trust-sensitive
extension is fired via `_onEnablementChanged`, and both TS
representations qualify). `ExtensionDescriptionRegistry._initialize()`
then logs "Extension '...' is already registered" and silently drops
the second entry -- which happens to leave TypeScript's *initial*
registration record inconsistent, permanently failing later
activation.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT LAYER (not enablementService, not the registry)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- `ExtensionsManager`/`ExtensionEnablementService`
  (extensionEnablementService.ts) intentionally dedupe only within one
  server (`areSameExtensions(...) && getExtensionManagementServer(a)
  === getExtensionManagementServer(b)`) -- correct behavior for actual
  multi-server installs; patching there would require it to *also*
  know which server should "win" for cross-server duplicates, logic
  that belongs with the servers themselves, not this consumer.
- `ExtensionDescriptionRegistry._initialize()` only ever *detects* a
  duplicate reactively (console.error + drop); it has no visibility
  into *why* two entries share an identifier, so it cannot safely
  decide which one is authoritative. Patching there is where a purely
  cosmetic "hide the error" fix would land -- explicitly rejected.
- The multiplexing point (`getInstalled()`) is the one place that
  actually knows a request spans multiple servers and can compare
  results before they are ever handed to a consumer as independent
  entries.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
THE FIX
--------------------------------------------------------------------

When both a remote and a web management server are configured
simultaneously, prefer the remote server's copy of any builtin
extension identifier the web server *also* reports, and drop the web
copy for that identifier only. Remote execution is strictly more
capable (Node.js APIs, filesystem access) than the web-worker
fallback, and the web copy exists specifically to cover the case where
NO remote connection is available -- which is false whenever a remote
server is configured at all. Non-builtin, user-installed extensions
that happen to be present on both servers (a much rarer, likely
intentional configuration) are left untouched, since only builtins
carry this specific "web variant exists purely as a remote fallback"
guarantee.

--- a/src/vs/workbench/services/extensionManagement/common/extensionManagementService.ts
+++ b/src/vs/workbench/services/extensionManagement/common/extensionManagementService.ts
@@ -NNN,7 +NNN,26 @@ export class ExtensionManagementService extends Disposable implements IWorkben
 	async getInstalled(type?: ExtensionType, profileLocation?: URI, productVersion?: IProductVersion): Promise<ILocalExtension[]> {
 		const result: ILocalExtension[] = [];
 		await Promise.all(this.servers.map(async server => {
 			const installed = await server.extensionManagementService.getInstalled(type, profileLocation, productVersion);
 			if (server === this.getWorkspaceExtensionsServer()) {
 				const workspaceExtensions = await this.getInstalledWorkspaceExtensions(true);
 				installed.push(...workspaceExtensions);
 			}
 			result.push(...installed);
 		}));
-		return result;
+		return this.dedupeBuiltinsAcrossRemoteAndWeb(result);
+	}
+
+	/**
+	 * When both a remote and a web extension management server are
+	 * configured at once (code-server's own deployment model: it
+	 * always runs a real remote extension host *and* always serves
+	 * VS Code Web's static web-extension assets), a builtin extension
+	 * with a `browser` entry point (e.g.
+	 * `vscode.typescript-language-features`) can be correctly reported
+	 * as "installed" by *both* servers. The web copy exists only as a
+	 * fallback for when no remote connection exists -- prefer the
+	 * remote copy and drop the web duplicate so downstream consumers
+	 * (`ExtensionsManager`, `AbstractExtensionService`) never see the
+	 * same logical builtin as two independent entries.
+	 */
+	private dedupeBuiltinsAcrossRemoteAndWeb(extensions: ILocalExtension[]): ILocalExtension[] {
+		const remoteServer = this.extensionManagementServerService.remoteExtensionManagementServer;
+		const webServer = this.extensionManagementServerService.webExtensionManagementServer;
+		if (!remoteServer || !webServer) {
+			return extensions;
+		}
+		const remoteBuiltinIds = new Set(
+			extensions
+				.filter(e => e.isBuiltin && this.extensionManagementServerService.getExtensionManagementServer(e) === remoteServer)
+				.map(e => e.identifier.id.toLowerCase())
+		);
+		return extensions.filter(e => {
+			const isWebDuplicateOfRemoteBuiltin =
+				e.isBuiltin
+				&& this.extensionManagementServerService.getExtensionManagementServer(e) === webServer
+				&& remoteBuiltinIds.has(e.identifier.id.toLowerCase());
+			return !isWebDuplicateOfRemoteBuiltin;
+		});
 	}

